Keep a scrolled-up terminal viewport where the user left it across a resize.

xterm's Buffer#resize advances ydisp once per row it drops off the top,
wherever the viewport happened to be. That is right while the viewport follows
the bottom - the cursor has to stay visible, so the content moves up by exactly
the height that was taken away - and wrong once the user has scrolled up to
read, where it drags the text out from under them by that same height. Nothing
puts it back, so this does.

A soft keyboard shrinking the panel is the cheapest way to see it and a sash
drag does it on desktop just as well, so this is not gated on the touch
capability. Columns are not part of it: Buffer#_reflow returns immediately
unless the column count changed.

An upstream defect, kept individually deletable: delete this patch the day
xterm anchors the viewport itself.

--- a/lib/vscode/src/vs/workbench/contrib/terminal/browser/xterm/xtermTerminal.ts
+++ b/lib/vscode/src/vs/workbench/contrib/terminal/browser/xterm/xtermTerminal.ts
@@ -541,7 +541,18 @@ export class XtermTerminal extends Disposable implements IXtermTerminal, IDetach
 
 	resize(columns: number, rows: number): void {
 		this._logService.debug('resizing', columns, rows);
+		// Composery: a viewport that was following the bottom must keep following it
+		// (xterm scrolls it for us); one the user had scrolled up must not move at all.
+		// Reading baseY rather than a flag keeps the alternate screen out of it for
+		// free - it has no scrollback, so viewportY and baseY are both always 0 there.
+		const before = this.raw.buffer.active;
+		const restoreTo = before.viewportY === before.baseY ? undefined : before.viewportY;
 		this.raw.resize(columns, rows);
+		if (restoreTo !== undefined) {
+			// scrollLines is clamped to the buffer, so a resize that ate the scrollback
+			// the viewport sat in lands as close to it as the buffer still allows.
+			this.raw.scrollLines(restoreTo - this.raw.buffer.active.viewportY);
+		}
 	}
 
 	updateLogLevel(): void {
